  "data": {
    "rev": 13,
    "xml": "<doc node='1418d617-de39' rev='13' sinceRev='1'><gap blocks='1'/><paragraph id='mv7z9mz74gt.20' rev='1' h='588da410'><date id='.21' value='2026-09-25'/><text id='.22'> · </text><mention id='.25' user='u_rDNeP9LtDuSqcJknv3N0Dg' name='David (Anthropic Super User with Root Privileges)' self='true'/></paragraph><gone id='.26' rev='2'/><paragraph id='.38' rev='2' h='d4b1ea57' heading='2'><text id='.39'>Overview</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.47' h='e4172e35'><text id='.48'>Thunderjar is David's side project for benchmarking coding-agent harnesses (Claude Code, OpenCode, Cursor, etc.) by running them headlessly against fixed tasks in git worktrees, then scoring the results. The goal is to compare harnesses, models, and prompt sets against each other on repeatable tasks, with enough statistical rigour (repeated iterations, pass rates) to trust the comparisons.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.440' h='7573df32'><text id='.441'>This document is a pre-spec: a consolidated snapshot of the design decisions, terminology, and open risks worked through across several conversations, intended as the input for a later full spec pass with an AI.</text></paragraph><gone id='.27' rev='3'/><paragraph id='.652' rev='3' h='4659d4d4' heading='2'><text id='.653'>Core Terminology</text></paragraph><list id='.669' h='8737da4d' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.670' h='d6840ab9'><paragraph id='.671' h='e3ac4565'><text id='.672'><bold>Task</bold></text><text id='.676'> — a starting commit plus a task instruction, defining what the agent is asked to do and from what state. A task also declares its </text><text id='.807'><bold>measurements</bold></text><text id='.819'>.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.820' h='4a9f31f0'><paragraph id='.821' h='46df7945'><text id='.822'><bold>Task instruction</bold></text><text id='.838'> — the text sent to the agent (</text><text id='.869'><code>ctx.taskInstruction</code></text><text id='.888'>). Distinct from a prompt set: the instruction directs the agent's work, a prompt set configures its environment.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.1001' h='f1cfd791'><paragraph id='.1002' h='057c7ae6'><text id='.1003'><bold>Harness</bold></text><text id='.1010'> — the agent tool being benchmarked (Claude Code, OpenCode, Cursor, etc.), registered with the model(s) it supports and a </text><text id='.1132'><code>cli</code></text><text id='.1135'> callback that invokes it.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.1161' h='d6a7831d'><paragraph id='.1162' h='943432e5'><text id='.1163'><bold>Prompt set</bold></text><text id='.1173'> — a named, reusable, reproducible action (a shell command) that overlays prompt files into the worktree before a run, e.g. </text><text id='.1297'><code>git checkout &lt;commit&gt; -- CLAUDE.md</code></text><text id='.1331'>. Referenced by ID from an experiment.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.1369' h='bfb2fa82'><paragraph id='.1370' h='644d56b6'><text id='.1371'><bold>Experiment</bold></text><text id='.1381'> — names a task and a set of </text><text id='.1410'><bold>variables</bold></text><text id='.1419'> (harnesses, models, prompt sets), then runs the task once for every permutation. E.g. 2 harnesses × 2 prompt sets = 4 runs.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.1543' h='36b8697a'><paragraph id='.1544' h='d4774f45'><text id='.1545'><bold>Run</bold></text><text id='.1548'> — a single execution of a task under one specific combination of variable values. Produces a </text><text id='.1642'><bold>result</bold></text><text id='.1648'>.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.1649' h='d1b43547'><paragraph id='.1650' h='a599be73'><text id='.1651'><bold>Measurer</bold></text><text id='.1659'> — a named, reusable, </text><text id='.1681'><italic>general-purpose</italic></text><text id='.1696'> implementation of how to observe something about a run (e.g. </text><text id='.1758'><code>grep</code></text><text id='.1762'>, </text><text id='.1764'><code>fileCreated</code></text><text id='.1775'>), registered once and parameterised by a config type and a value type.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.1846' h='fff5a997'><paragraph id='.1847' h='5e057fae'><text id='.1848'><bold>Measurement</bold></text><text id='.1859'> — a task-level declaration pairing a measurer with specific config (e.g. </text><text id='.1933'><code>grep</code></text><text id='.1937'> with glob </text><text id='.1948'><code>output/**</code></text><text id='.1957'> and pattern </text><text id='.1970'><code>snerk</code></text><text id='.1975'>). Many measurements can reuse the same measurer with different config. A measurement returns a </text><text id='.2071'><code>MeasurementResult&lt;T&gt;</code></text><text id='.2091'>: </text><text id='.2093'><code>measured</code></text><text id='.2101'> (with data), </text><text id='.2115'><code>skipped</code></text><text id='.2122'>, or </text><text id='.2127'><code>erroredWhileMeasuring</code></text><text id='.2148'> — not a simple pass/fail verdict.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.2182' h='fed51616'><paragraph id='.2183' h='2c6ae0a5'><text id='.2184'><bold>Result</bold></text><text id='.2190'> — the outcome of a run: the set of measurement values produced, stored with full variable context so runs are comparable across experiments.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.2331' h='256daf42'><text id='.2332'>Measurer vs. measurement is a type-vs-instance distinction: </text><text id='.2392'><code>grep</code></text><text id='.2396'> is a measurer (general capability); \"does </text><text id='.2439'><code>output/**</code></text><text id='.2448'> contain </text><text id='.2457'><code>TS-ignore</code></text><text id='.2466'>\" is a measurement (a specific config of that measurer).</text></paragraph><gone id='.28' rev='4'/><paragraph id='.2522' rev='4' h='1ec7b4cf' heading='2'><text id='.2523'>Determinism &amp; Versioning via Content Hashing</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.2567' h='83c3bfb2'><text id='.2568'>Both prompt sets and measurements need stable identity over time so historical runs stay comparable, but freezing content by convention (\"never edit </text><text id='.2717'><code>snerk</code></text><text id='.2722'>, bump to </text><text id='.2732'><code>snerk-v2</code></text><text id='.2740'> instead\") relies on human discipline. The resolved approach instead uses </text><text id='.2814'><bold>content hashing</bold></text><text id='.2829'>:</text></paragraph><list id='.2830' h='63fdc018' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.2831' h='298c6afc'><paragraph id='.2832' h='f6d997dc'><text id='.2833'>At run time, compute a content hash of a prompt set's actual output (or source file), and store name + hash together as the true identity in run metadata.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.2987' h='f6cee378'><paragraph id='.2988' h='d13bf186'><text id='.2989'>The same applies to measurements: hash the measurer logic / config, and store a growing map of </text><text id='.3084'><code>measurer-name + hash → result</code></text><text id='.3113'> per run.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.3122' h='b9c1f294'><paragraph id='.3123' h='0de2006f'><text id='.3124'>This lets comparisons be done honestly: runs sharing name </text><text id='.3182'><italic>and</italic></text><text id='.3185'> hash are genuinely comparable; a later run with the same name but a different hash is flagged as different content, not silently pooled with earlier data.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.3340' h='0ccea8bd'><paragraph id='.3341' h='026dd9ae'><text id='.3342'>It also gives you drift </text><text id='.3366'><italic>detection</italic></text><text id='.3375'> for free, and can be extended to drift </text><text id='.3415'><italic>rejection</italic></text><text id='.3424'> (refuse a run if its hash doesn't match a previously pinned hash for that name), without requiring the freeze-and-rename discipline.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.3557' h='d1f3e662'><paragraph id='.3558' h='c7924976'><text id='.3559'><bold>Caveat</bold></text><text id='.3565'>: this only works cleanly for prompt sets / measurers whose output is deterministic. A prompt set or measurer that shells out to something non-deterministic (an API call, a timestamp) will produce hash \"drift\" that isn't a real content change — treated as user error / a known limitation, same as model-delegation nondeterminism is treated as an observed outcome rather than a controlled variable (see Test Matrix Model).</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.3986' h='2ddc4131'><text id='.3987'><bold>Reuse across tasks was considered and rejected.</bold></text><text id='.4034'> Named, shared measurements (e.g. one </text><text id='.4072'><code>contains-ts-ignore</code></text><text id='.4090'> measurement referenced by many tasks) create a many-to-many coupling problem: changing a shared measurement later is blocked by every task depending on it. Resolved decision: </text><text id='.4266'><bold>no shared/referenced measurements — copy-paste per task.</bold></text><text id='.4322'> Each task owns its measurement config outright; duplication is an acceptable trade-off for avoiding coupling, especially at current scale. If duplication becomes painful later, a config-generating helper function (called at config-authoring time, producing inline config per task) could reduce repetition in source without reintroducing runtime coupling.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.4677' h='20ab6e48'><text id='.4678'><bold>Filename non-determinism.</bold></text><text id='.4703'> Agents may not name output files consistently (</text><text id='.4751'><code>is-prime.ts</code></text><text id='.4762'> vs </text><text id='.4766'><code>isPrime.ts</code></text><text id='.4776'> vs </text><text id='.4780'><code>prime.ts</code></text><text id='.4788'>). Rather than solving this with fuzzy matching, the resolved pattern is: keep measurement globs broad (</text><text id='.4892'><code>output/**</code></text><text id='.4901'> or </text><text id='.4905'><code>output/*.ts</code></text><text id='.4916'>) when the check is about </text><text id='.4942'><italic>behaviour</italic></text><text id='.4951'>, not naming. A narrow, exact-filename glob is then a deliberate signal that correct naming is itself part of what's being tested, not an accident of an overly specific check.</text></paragraph><gone id='.29' rev='5'/><paragraph id='.5126' rev='5' h='70718ae3' heading='2'><text id='.5127'>Retroactive Measurement Workflow</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.5159' h='681851bc'><text id='.5160'>A key design goal: adding a new measurement to a task should never require re-running the agent. Because full run state is preserved (see Container Preservation), a new measurer can be applied to an old run's final filesystem state directly:</text></paragraph><list id='.5401' h='ef3340a9' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.5402' h='86938a09'><paragraph id='.5403' h='1efa6fb4'><text id='.5404'>Restore the run's container.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.5432' h='33fa1dd8'><paragraph id='.5433' h='bc53e55e'><text id='.5434'>Execute the new measurer's check against the already-existing final state.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.5508' h='9a3e561e'><paragraph id='.5509' h='9930d154'><text id='.5510'>Append the result to that run's stored result map — existing measurement results are untouched, since their measurer/config hashes haven't changed.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.5657' h='f1c3f1a6'><paragraph id='.5658' h='2bfad214'><text id='.5659'>Genuinely </text><text id='.5669'><italic>redefining</italic></text><text id='.5679'> an existing measurement (not adding a new one) is caught automatically by the hash mismatch, rather than relying on nobody having touched the file.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.5827' h='904232a1'><text id='.5828'><bold>Developer-experience consequence:</bold></text><text id='.5861'> this decouples two iteration loops that are normally conflated and made expensive together — running the agent, and writing/debugging the measurer itself. Writing a measurer becomes a fast, local, deterministic loop: restore a container once, then iterate on the measurer's logic (grep pattern, glob, assertion) against that same fixed snapshot as many times as needed, with no tokens spent and no agent variance. This is effectively test-driven development against a fixture, where the fixture (the agent's run) was produced once, expensively, and is then free to reuse.</text></paragraph><gone id='.30' rev='6'/><paragraph id='.6433' rev='6' h='d1e04c76' heading='2'><text id='.6434'>Container Preservation &amp; Forensics Strategy</text></paragraph><list id='.6477' h='3800f5d9' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.6478' h='1fc4bb66'><paragraph id='.6479' h='3feb3f65'><text id='.6480'>Use </text><text id='.6484'><code>docker commit</code></text><text id='.6497'> to snapshot a finished test-run container's full filesystem (git history, session logs, OTel traces, output files) into a tagged image, pushed to a registry. This is a named, standard pattern (\"container forensics\") used in security incident response, and is mountable read-only for later inspection.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.6798' h='2eb8364e'><paragraph id='.6799' h='46179592'><text id='.6800'>CI-world convention typically differs — selective artifact archiving (logs/screenshots/traces) rather than whole-container snapshots. </text><text id='.6934'><bold>Recommended hybrid:</bold></text><text id='.6953'> default to lightweight artifact extraction (JSON result, OTel trace, session JSONL) for </text><text id='.7042'><italic>every</italic></text><text id='.7047'> run; reserve full </text><text id='.7066'><code>docker commit</code></text><text id='.7079'> snapshots for failing or flagged runs only, to manage cost/storage.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.7147' h='b80ee36b'><paragraph id='.7148' h='b9be9064'><text id='.7149'><bold>Registry storage economics:</bold></text><text id='.7176'> registries bill per unique compressed layer (~$0.10/GB-month on AWS ECR), not per image. Since </text><text id='.7272'><code>docker commit</code></text><text id='.7285'> only adds one new layer on top of a shared, unchanged base image, thousands of run-snapshots sharing a base image cost far less than naive per-image estimates suggest — plausibly a few dollars/month for tens of thousands of small-diff runs.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.7526' h='1ef6e848'><paragraph id='.7527' h='e0e4b997'><text id='.7528'><bold>Tagging scheme:</bold></text><text id='.7543'> test-case-id + model + run-timestamp/UUID, to support later systematic pruning.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.7623' h='437171ee'><paragraph id='.7624' h='cfb362ac'><text id='.7625'><bold>Restore path:</bold></text><text id='.7638'> </text><text id='.7639'><code>docker pull</code></text><text id='.7650'> the tagged image, then attach via VSCode Dev Containers / Remote-Containers for direct filesystem and git browsing of that historical run.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><gone id='.31' rev='7'/><paragraph id='.7789' rev='7' h='73316190' heading='2'><text id='.7790'>Data Architecture: Two-Tier Storage</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.7825' h='c5bcd78e'><text id='.7826'><bold>1. Metadata / summary store</bold></text><text id='.7853'> (small, queryable — SQLite locally, Postgres or S3+manifest in CI):</text></paragraph><list id='.7921' h='94dde4d4' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.7922' h='aad2c5e6'><paragraph id='.7923' h='af215a55'><text id='.7924'>JSON run summaries: pass/fail, cost, tokens, duration, per-model breakdown, keyed by run ID.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.8016' h='607aeed2'><paragraph id='.8017' h='d701afd1'><text id='.8018'>Lightweight OTel trace + session JSONL transcript, extracted at commit-time, keyed by iteration ID.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.8117' h='1b53731c'><paragraph id='.8118' h='b856c560'><text id='.8119'>Enables a \"telemetry view\" without pulling full containers.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.8178' h='b0feb9a8'><text id='.8179'><bold>2. Container artifact store</bold></text><text id='.8206'> (large, blob-like — registry):</text></paragraph><list id='.8237' h='655d6528' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.8238' h='32308058'><paragraph id='.8239' h='afc41f82'><text id='.8240'>Full </text><text id='.8245'><code>docker commit</code></text><text id='.8258'>-tagged images per iteration ID, pushed then pruned locally.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.8318' h='f5e9b318'><paragraph id='.8319' h='7564de12'><text id='.8320'>Enables \"full restore\" via </text><text id='.8347'><code>docker pull</code></text><text id='.8358'> + Dev Containers attach.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.8383' h='2d5e9e8f'><text id='.8384'><bold>Config needs:</bold></text><text id='.8397'> metadata backend connection string, registry endpoint, differentiated retention policies per tier (container images likely need a shorter TTL than the cheap trace/metadata blobs).</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.8577' h='af024182'><text id='.8578'><bold>Related prior decision (from an earlier session):</bold></text><text id='.8627'> for git-branch scaling, store binary patches + result JSON in a runs directory rather than one git branch per run, to avoid GitHub's ~5,000 branch soft limit. Use SQLite/DuckDB for pass-rate queries, and patches/transcripts for \"what did the agent actually do\" queries — consistent with the two-tier split above.</text></paragraph><gone id='.32' rev='8'/><paragraph id='.8940' rev='8' h='534e2009' heading='2'><text id='.8941'>Telemetry &amp; Observability Findings</text></paragraph><list id='.8975' h='a9f225a0' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.8976' h='441f77c9'><paragraph id='.8977' h='183f1c38'><text id='.8978'><bold>Native OTel support varies by harness.</bold></text><text id='.9016'> Claude Code, Codex, and Qwen Code have native OTel support (</text><text id='.9077'><code>CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY=1</code></text><text id='.9107'> + </text><text id='.9110'><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code></text><text id='.9137'> for Claude Code; tracing is beta). OpenCode does not — community plugins only.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.9216' h='bde6877f'><paragraph id='.9217' h='c0e725f3'><text id='.9218'><bold>Headless structured output</bold></text><text id='.9244'> via </text><text id='.9249'><code>claude -p \"...\" --output-format json</code></text><text id='.9285'> returns clean JSON (</text><text id='.9306'><code>result</code></text><text id='.9312'>, </text><text id='.9314'><code>total_cost_usd</code></text><text id='.9328'>, </text><text id='.9330'><code>session_id</code></text><text id='.9340'>, </text><text id='.9342'><code>usage</code></text><text id='.9347'> with token breakdown including cache creation/read tokens) — sufficient for Thunderjar without needing the Node SDK. Note: the SDK's </text><text id='.9481'><code>modelUsage</code></text><text id='.9491'> field is more accurate than plain </text><text id='.9526'><code>usage</code></text><text id='.9531'> once sub-agents are involved (plain </text><text id='.9568'><code>usage</code></text><text id='.9573'> has an undercounting bug in that case).</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.9613' h='187fbd06'><paragraph id='.9614' h='73824848'><text id='.9615'><bold>Prompt caching:</bold></text><text id='.9630'> cache write ≈1.25× cost, cache read ≈0.1× cost; default TTL 5 minutes (1 hour via </text><text id='.9713'><code>ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1</code></text><text id='.9739'>); requires exact prefix match.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.9770' h='dbcb8b97'><paragraph id='.9771' h='1e15decb'><text id='.9772'><bold>Skills:</bold></text><text id='.9779'> frontmatter (name + description) is preloaded into context at session start; the full </text><text id='.9866'><code>SKILL.md</code></text><text id='.9874'> body is pulled in via a normal file read on invocation; bundled scripts are just files the model invokes via its existing bash/tool capability. Claude Code currently lacks skill-invocation-name telemetry (open GitHub issue #35319); Claude Cowork's OTel integration does track this. Workaround: skill markdown reads show up in ordinary PreToolUse/PostToolUse hook payloads as file-read tool calls with the skill's path visible, so usage is reconstructable indirectly via path-matching.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.10359' h='dec4dec2'><paragraph id='.10360' h='c91f6c7e'><text id='.10361'><bold>Turns vs. reasoning:</bold></text><text id='.10381'> a single API response can contain multiple content block types (thinking, text, tool_use) in one turn — that's one request/response, not multiple hops. \"Extended thinking\" only controls the size of the thinking block within that one call. True multi-hop/multi-turn only happens when tool use forces a new request (harness executes the tool client-side, sends the result back as a new API call) — this produces the real nested trace structure, and sub-agent delegation nests further under a specific turn's span. OTel span-level model attribution makes it possible to see which model handled which sub-agent turn.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.10994' h='3cbd7248'><paragraph id='.10995' h='e369e690'><text id='.10996'><bold>Thinking content is not exposed via native telemetry</bold></text><text id='.11048'>, even with full content-logging flags enabled — deliberately redacted (open GitHub issue #46118 requesting an </text><text id='.11159'><code>OTEL_LOG_THINKING</code></text><text id='.11176'>-style flag). Full reasoning content </text><text id='.11213'><italic>is</italic></text><text id='.11215'> available in on-disk session transcripts (JSONL files under </text><text id='.11276'><code>.claude/projects/...</code></text><text id='.11296'>, filename = session ID). Session ID is a shared correlation key present both as an OTel span attribute and as the transcript filename, enabling scripted correlation from an anomalous trace/span to its full reasoning transcript.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.11524' h='733f422d'><paragraph id='.11525' h='43900a8b'><text id='.11526'><bold>Open question, flagged but not yet researched:</bold></text><text id='.11572'> on a second/later invocation trigger within the same session, does the model re-read an already-loaded skill file, or rely on it still being in context?</text></paragraph></listItem></list><gone id='.33' rev='9'/><paragraph id='.11725' rev='9' h='08f94a21' heading='2'><text id='.11726'>Isolation &amp; Safety</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.11744' h='cdd6160d'><text id='.11745'><bold>Cache isolation for parallel runs.</bold></text><text id='.11779'> </text><text id='.11780'><code>DISABLE_PROMPT_CACHING=1</code></text><text id='.11804'> was rejected — it kills caching entirely, which loses the benefit of caching </text><text id='.11882'><italic>within</italic></text><text id='.11888'> a run. Prompt caching lives server-side on Anthropic's infrastructure, so Docker container isolation alone does </text><text id='.12001'><italic>not</italic></text><text id='.12004'> prevent cross-run cache hits if prompt prefixes match. The correct mechanism is Anthropic's </text><text id='.12097'><code>prompt-cache-key</code></text><text id='.12113'> request header, namespaced per run (e.g. a UUID per test run) — this guarantees no cross-run cache contamination while preserving in-run caching benefits.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.12268' h='f49b2ca9'><text id='.12269'><bold>External side effects.</bold></text><text id='.12291'> Real API keys/credentials (e.g. GitHub tokens for PR creation) should not be given to test containers — runs must stay ephemeral and side-effect-free to be safely parallelizable. For test cases that specifically need to exercise an external-integration agentic flow (e.g. \"create a PR\"), the resolved approach is dependency injection via MCP: register a fake/mock MCP server (e.g. a fake GitHub MCP) that records calls and returns canned responses, swapped in for the real MCP server depending on run context. This also creates a new assertion surface — asserting on the exact MCP calls received, not just git/file state.</text></paragraph><gone id='.34' rev='10'/><paragraph id='.12913' rev='10' h='ca4c536c' heading='2'><text id='.12914'>Test Matrix Model</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.12931' h='f165e5d4'><text id='.12932'>A test case's identity is: </text><text id='.12959'><bold>code commit + prompt commit + model (the harness's root model only) + harness version + task instruction.</bold></text><text id='.13064'> A delegated sub-agent's model is an </text><text id='.13101'><italic>observed outcome</italic></text><text id='.13117'>, not a controlled parameter — it can vary run to run and is recorded, not fixed.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.13198' h='4ed4b16a'><text id='.13199'><bold>Iterations</bold></text><text id='.13209'> are repeated runs of the same matrix point, used for pass-rate statistics.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.13284' h='8af8c35b'><text id='.13285'><bold>Standard/free assertions</bold></text><text id='.13309'> apply to every run automatically: final text block, commit list (existence, messages, hashes), changed files diff. </text><text id='.13425'><bold>Custom measurements</bold></text><text id='.13444'> (\"measurers\") are opt-in per task, e.g. database state checks, unit-test-style checks against generated output.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.13556' h='d056c0a9'><text id='.13557'>A pluggable/plugin architecture for measurer types was proposed: a core, universal set plus optional domain-specific packs (e.g. DB assertions), so Thunderjar can grow to cover other people's use cases without bloating the core.</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.13785' h='0b7bb9dc'><text id='.13786'><bold>Rejected alternative:</bold></text><text id='.13807'> treating \"assertion/measurer version\" as a formal sixth matrix axis was considered too complex/messy. The resolved model instead keeps each task's config (including its measurements) in one file that, once used, is effectively versioned via content hashing rather than a separate tracked axis (see Determinism &amp; Versioning). Confirmed against the actual </text><text id='.14162'><code>ThunderJarConfig</code></text><text id='.14178'> source: measurements are inline data within a task registration in the same config file as everything else, so there is no separate \"assertion commit\" to track.</text></paragraph><gone id='.35' rev='11'/><paragraph id='.14339' rev='11' h='35adf5ac' heading='2'><text id='.14340'>CLI UX &amp; Run Provenance</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.14363' h='e5674d66'><text id='.14364'>Two distinct UX modes need different architecture:</text></paragraph><list id='.14414' h='f8001e01' kind='ordered'><listItem id='.14415' h='d5ca274d'><paragraph id='.14416' h='c2327e13'><text id='.14417'><bold>CI / non-interactive mode</bold></text><text id='.14442'> — scriptable, exit-code-driven, machine-readable JSON output.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.14504' h='48062bc4'><paragraph id='.14505' h='ed05afc6'><text id='.14506'><bold>Interactive / explore mode</bold></text><text id='.14532'> — for browsing past runs, drilling into a specific iteration, triggering full restores. Likely warrants a proper TUI (e.g. built with </text><text id='.14667'><code>ink</code></text><text id='.14670'>) as a separate subcommand (</text><text id='.14698'><code>thunderjar explore</code></text><text id='.14716'>), rather than chaining single-shot commands. Good dependency injection should keep both paths equally testable regardless of this split.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.14853' h='713f0af2'><text id='.14854'><bold>Local developer iteration loops:</bold></text></paragraph><list id='.14886' h='49033cfa' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.14887' h='1c2a63d9'><paragraph id='.14888' h='c284ec6c'><text id='.14889'>Fast single-iteration loop while actively tuning a prompt/skill or the measurements themselves — low iteration count by default.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.15017' h='fda9646a'><paragraph id='.15018' h='e0fe61e1'><text id='.15019'>Confidence-check loop once satisfied — deliberately bump to 50–100 iterations to verify pass rate before considering the work done.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.15150' h='84470849'><paragraph id='.15151' h='c35f2910'><text id='.15152'>CLI should default to a low iteration count with an explicit flag to raise it, rather than defaulting to expensive batches during fast iteration.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph id='.15297' h='c550844f'><text id='.15298'><bold>Provenance/retention tagging:</bold></text><text id='.15327'> local dev runs are tagged separately from CI runs and treated as ephemeral/throwaway by default (possibly a separate, never-synced local SQLite file). CI runs are canonical — pushed to the shared metadata store and registry with long-term retention. Explore/restore tooling filters to CI-verified runs by default, showing local runs only on request. This is lifecycle/provenance tagging, not authentication.</text></paragraph><gone id='.36' rev='12'/><paragraph id='.15735' rev='12' h='0bb5914b' heading='2'><text id='.15736'>Risks &amp; Open Questions</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.15758' h='a214d903'><text id='.15759'><bold>Risks (raised and still standing):</bold></text></paragraph><list id='.15793' h='c1a4848c' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.15794' h='78571bf9'><paragraph id='.15795' h='1016b920'><text id='.15796'><bold>Determinism is a load-bearing assumption.</bold></text><text id='.15837'> Hash-based versioning for prompt sets and measurers only holds if the shell commands behind them (</text><text id='.15936'><code>onWorktreeCreate</code></text><text id='.15952'>, prompt set </text><text id='.15965'><code>apply</code></text><text id='.15970'> commands) are actually deterministic. Nothing stops someone writing one that hits the network or reads a timestamp — the system can't detect that violation; it will just produce hash drift that looks like a bug rather than what it is. Currently accepted as a known limitation / user error, not solved.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.16272' h='8c7099d7'><paragraph id='.16273' h='c83af34d'><text id='.16274'><bold>Container snapshots may not fully capture runtime state.</bold></text><text id='.16330'> The restore-and-backfill workflow assumes the container filesystem is a sufficient proxy for \"what happened.\" That holds for file-based measurements, but a measurer that needs the actual runtime (e.g. importing and unit-testing generated code) also needs a faithful runtime environment — correct language/runtime version, installed dependencies, anything the agent's process touched outside files. Base-image drift over time could mean a measurement written today doesn't restore-and-run correctly against a three-week-old container.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.16864' h='b1744def'><paragraph id='.16865' h='6d4f811a'><text id='.16866'><bold>Open, unanswered from reviewing the actual </bold></text><text id='.16909'><bold><code>ThunderJarConfig</code></bold></text><text id='.16925'><bold> source:</bold></text></paragraph><list id='.16933' h='91faa8e8' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.16934' h='606dd7d4'><paragraph id='.16935' h='29089cc4'><text id='.16936'><code>commitHash: 'HEAD'</code></text><text id='.16954'> currently appears in experiment configs but it's unconfirmed whether commit-checkout is actually wired up anywhere in </text><text id='.17073'><code>ThunderJarConfig</code></text><text id='.17089'> — or just a literal placeholder string right now.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.17139' h='715818d5'><paragraph id='.17140' h='9f39dc65'><text id='.17141'>No visible validation that an experiment's requested model (in </text><text id='.17204'><code>variables.harnesses</code></text><text id='.17223'>) is actually among the models declared for that harness in </text><text id='.17283'><code>registerHarness</code></text><text id='.17298'> — </text><comment id='4a84e58b-a009'/><text id='.17301'>the two lists could drift apart silently.</text></paragraph></listItem></list></listItem></list><paragraph id='.17342' h='d04f5061'><text id='.17343'><bold>Explicitly deferred as nice-to-have, not yet designed (see also next section):</bold></text></paragraph><list id='.17421' h='2b9ee1f4' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.17422' h='b127da35'><paragraph id='.17423' h='e1e06964'><text id='.17424'>Flakiness/retry handling for Thunderjar's own infra calls (Docker/registry failures) vs. genuine assertion failures.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.17540' h='9c26b7c4'><paragraph id='.17541' h='4747ed9b'><text id='.17542'>Cost/rate-limit governance — e.g. a cost preview before firing a large batch of iterations.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><gone id='.37' rev='13'/><paragraph id='.17633' rev='13' h='3577a232' heading='2'><text id='.17634'>Explicitly Deferred / Out of Scope</text></paragraph><paragraph id='.17668' h='f1220c05'><text id='.17669'>These were raised during discussion and deliberately not designed now, current goal is a proof of concept:</text></paragraph><list id='.17775' h='bde4c16c' kind='bullet'><listItem id='.17776' h='45698b4e'><paragraph id='.17777' h='07b4dc0c'><text id='.17778'>Flakiness/retry handling for Thunderjar's own infrastructure calls (Docker, registry) as distinct from genuine test assertion failures.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.17913' h='948b7c3a'><paragraph id='.17914' h='3b0e5a91'><text id='.17915'>Cost / rate-limit governance (e.g. previewing cost before a large iteration batch).</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem id='.17998' h='9ea5873e'><paragraph id='.17999' h='18088777'><text id='.18000'>Whether a skill file already loaded into context gets re-read on a later invocation within the same session, or relied upon from context — flagged for later, not yet researched.</text></paragraph></listItem></list></doc>",
